.docname {Security Model}
.docauthor {BakingRL}
.include {docs}
.doclang {en}

BakingRL uses a host-enforced permission model. Plugin code is not trusted.

## Host Authority

The Rust host owns:

- package installation and bundle extraction;
- manifest parsing and effective permissions;
- runtime API compatibility decisions;
- telemetry ingestion;
- registry reads and writes;
- service RPC;
- component imports;
- network access for connectors;
- storage access.

SDK helpers improve authoring ergonomics but are not a security boundary.

## Package Boundaries

Packages cannot import each other directly. Cross-package access must go through
host-mediated imports and permissions.

Examples:

- A visual loads a component with `context.components.load("package/export")`.
- A visual calls a service with `context.services.call("package/service", method, input)`.
- A service writes only under permitted `plugin.<package-id>.*` registry keys.
- A connector can call only declared HTTP or WebSocket hosts.
- A private configuration visual can render only inside its package
  configuration page.

## Bundle Safety

The host rejects bundles with:

- absolute paths;
- `../` path traversal;
- symlinks;
- oversized files;
- oversized total uncompressed size;
- hash mismatches;
- invalid Ed25519 signatures.

## Trust State

Hash and signature files are package metadata. They help the host and future
marketplace reason about integrity, but they do not make plugin code trusted.
Remote trust policy belongs to the BakingRL host and marketplace design, not to
plugin packages.

## Practical Guidance

- Request the smallest permission set possible.
- Keep secrets outside package source.
- Treat all telemetry and settings input as untrusted.
- Prefer explicit service methods over broad registry writes.
